    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper presents a high-resolution palaeoclimate record from northern Finland revealing that the Eemian interglacial was characterized by decoupled seasonal trends, with cooling summers and warming winters, and was interrupted by two abrupt cooling events linked to disturbances in North Atlantic oceanic circulation.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic oceanic circulation regime",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "These abrupt events parallel shifts in marine proxy data, linked to disturbances in the North Atlantic oceanic circulation regime.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Tunturi cooling event",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Finland",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "Here we present a high-resolution (sub-centennial) record of Eemian palaeoclimate from northern Finland, with multi-model reconstructions for July and January air temperature. In contrast with the mid-latitudes of Europe, our data show decoupled seasonal trends with falling July and rising January temperatures over the Eemian, due to orbital and oceanic forcings. This leads to an oceanic Late-Eemian climate, consistent with an earlier hypothesis of glacial inception in Europe. The interglacial is further intersected by two strong cooling and drying events. These abrupt events parallel shifts in marine proxy data, linked to disturbances in the North Atlantic oceanic circulation regime.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "V\u00e4rri\u00f6 event",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Finland",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "Between depths 17.40-17.05 m, i.e., within Zone VI, shifts are recorded in the examined proxies. This abrupt cooling and drying event is here defined the V\u00e4rri\u00f6 event. This event has a relatively muted signal in the terrestrial pollen record, however the initial peak (two samples at 17.37-17.39 m) is seen as a sharp minimum in total tree pollen, with the ratio of arboreal to non-arboreal pollen (mean = 2.9) below the lowest value reached during the Tunturi event (4.8) indicating the opening of tree cover, while a maximum in Sphagnum suggests the extension of the wetland zone. A more distinct and longer lasting signal for the V\u00e4rri\u00f6 event is found in the aquatic proxy record (see Discussion).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Records from the Eirik Drift, south of Greenland, show a concurrent cooling17, together with reductions in North Atlantic Deep Water formation, inferred from low benthic \u03b413C excursions, suggesting a weakening of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC; Fig. 4d)15.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "glacial inception",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Europe",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "This leads to an oceanic Late-Eemian climate, consistent with an earlier hypothesis of glacial inception in Europe.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "LOVECLIM1.3",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "transient",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Late-Eemian maxima in AMOC strength4,47 and Nordic Seas surface temperature52,53 are also predicted by transient modelling when including prescribed Northern Hemisphere ice-sheet dynamics, due to an uptake of freshwater by the expanding ice sheets, with the first-order changes in modelled AMOC matching the proxy data4,15.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
